          "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"PERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"CORE_ONLY\",\"rationale\":\"Isaiah 9:15 expressly identifies “the tail” as “the prophet that teacheth lies,” and verse 16 immediately links such leadership with causing people to err and destroying those led. This gives the book’s central identification stronger direct support than the counter-reading that tail primarily means low rank. Deuteronomy establishes the head/tail hierarchy, but that hierarchy is compatible with Isaiah’s more specific identification rather than displacing it. The entry overreaches, however, when it treats details in Revelation 9 and Exodus 4 as established instances of the same meaning: those texts do not themselves connect the tails with lying prophets.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"In symbolic use, the tail identifies the lying prophet or misleading leader whose teaching causes followers to err and be destroyed.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"Deuteronomy’s head-and-tail covenant contrast supplies the rank structure behind Isaiah’s identification.\",\"The dragon casting down stars with its tail depicts people being destroyed through misleading leadership.\",\"The serpent-like tails that “had heads” depict the liar elevated into authority—“the liar enthroned.”\",\"Moses’ command to seize the serpent by its tail deliberately invokes the same lying-prophet symbolism.\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[\"Revelation 9:19’s tails “had heads” specifically means “the liar enthroned.” The verse describes serpent-like tails equipped with heads that inflict injury, but does not identify those heads as enthroned false prophets.\",\"Exodus 4:4’s instruction to seize the serpent by its tail intentionally invokes the lying-prophet symbolism. The excerpt records where Moses grasped the serpent but supplies no interpretation of that location.\"],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"The chapter’s identification of Revelation 12’s stars specifically as saints is not established by the glossary entry’s cited excerpts.\",\"The chapter’s larger rod-priesthood-serpent-restored-priesthood typology goes substantially beyond what Exodus 4:2-4 explicitly interprets.\",\"The chapter’s chazaq wordplay between grasping the serpent and hardening Pharaoh’s heart is suggestive but does not establish the glossary meaning of tail.\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"Tail primarily signifies subordination, inferiority, or the trailing position opposite the head. Isaiah 9 locally assigns the lying prophet to that inferior member, while Revelation’s tails are destructive bodily instruments and Exodus 4 uses an ordinary serpent’s tail without a fixed symbolic meaning.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"Tail denotes subordinate or inferior position; the lying prophet is only Isaiah’s context-specific occupant of that position, not a uniform symbolic definition.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The counter denies that Isaiah’s explicit equation should govern the other symbolic tail passages, treating it as a local application of a broader rank metaphor.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Deuteronomy 28:13 and 28:44 explicitly explain head and tail through being above or beneath and through lender-borrower dominance.\",\"Revelation 9:19 emphasizes that the tails possess power, resemble serpents, have heads, and cause injury, naturally supporting destructive capacity rather than false teaching.\",\"Exodus 4:4 can be read as a narrative instruction about controlling a transformed serpent, without symbolic significance assigned to its tail.\",\"Biblical uses of the same image can vary by context, so Isaiah’s explicit local equation need not function as a universal symbolic dictionary entry.\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It rests its core on Scripture’s only supplied explicit personal decode of tail: “the prophet that teacheth lies.”\",\"Isaiah 9:16 immediately explains the operative effect: misleading leaders cause error and the led are destroyed.\",\"It integrates Isaiah’s deceptive leadership with the head/tail hierarchy in Deuteronomy rather than leaving the two usages wholly unrelated.\",\"The dragon’s tail casting stars down has a meaningful structural resemblance to Isaiah’s sequence of misleading leadership followed by the destruction of those led.\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It gives the most direct reading of Deuteronomy’s above/beneath and lender/borrower contrasts.\",\"It accounts for Revelation 9:19’s anatomical and injurious details without converting each feature into a leadership symbol.\",\"It avoids assigning symbolic significance to the point at which Moses grasped the serpent when Exodus supplies none.\",\"It better accommodates contextual variation among prophetic, apocalyptic, and narrative passages.\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[],\"comparative_winner\":\"BOOK\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"Isaiah 9:15-16\",\"Deuteronomy 28:13,44\",\"Revelation 12:4\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"The evidence supports the lying-prophet identification most strongly in Isaiah; its uniform transfer to every tail image remains underdetermined.\",\"Revelation 12:4 does not itself state that the dragon’s tail represents deceptive teaching, even though Isaiah 9:15-16 supplies a coherent intertextual reading.\",\"The rank and deception senses may be complementary in Isaiah rather than reducible to one universal meaning.\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
      ],
